<?xml version="1.0" encoding="ISO-8859-1"?>
<wps:ProcessDescriptions xmlns:wps="http://www.opengis.net/wps/1.0.0" xmlns:ows="http://www.opengis.net/ows/1.1" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.opengis.net/wps/1.0.0 schemas/wps/1.0.0/wpsDescribeProcess_response.xsd" service="WPS" version="1.0.0" xml:lang="en-US">
	<ProcessDescription wps:processVersion="1" storeSupported="true" statusSupported="false">
		<ows:Identifier>MiraMon:IsoMDE:1</ows:Identifier>
		<ows:Title>IsoMDE</ows:Title>
		<ows:Abstract>This program allows digital terrain models (DTMs) to be generated from all sorts of isolines, and may use other additional information like lines showing valleys, hillcrests, etc. Although the most typical use of IsoMDE is for obtaining Digital Terrain Models (DTMs) that describe the topography based on conventional contour lines, the program allows any quasi-continuous surface to be generated from any sort of isoline. For example, IsoMDE can be used to interpolate between isotherms, isobars, isochrones, isobaths, etc. to obtain respectively quasi-continuous raster layers of temperature, pressure, access times, bathymetry, etc.</ows:Abstract>
		<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Title:Generate Digital Terrain Models from contour lines (Isolines)"/>
		<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Abstract:This program allows digital terrain models (DTMs) to be generated from all sorts of isolines, and may use other additional information like lines showing valleys, hillcrests, etc. Although the most typical use of IsoMDE is for obtaining Digital Terrain Models (DTMs) that describe the topography based on conventional contour lines, the program allows any quasi-continuous surface to be generated from any sort of isoline. For example, IsoMDE can be used to interpolate between isotherms, isobars, isochrones, isobaths, etc. to obtain respectively quasi-continuous raster layers of temperature, pressure, access times, bathymetry, etc."/>
		<DataInputs>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>FOri</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Isoline ARC vector</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Is the  Is the isoline ARC vector. It may contain lines of valleys. It may contain lines of valleys.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param01"/>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input>
				<ows:Identifier>FRes</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Raster output</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Is the raster output from the interpolation.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param02"/>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Xmin</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>X minimum</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Corner coordinates of the output raster. If "x x x x" is input then the extents of the Fori file will be used.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param03"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#float">float</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Xmax</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>X maximum</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Corner coordinates of the output raster. If "x x x x" is input then the extents of the Fori file will be used.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param04"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#float">float</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Ymin</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Y minimum</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Corner coordinates of the output raster. If "x x x x" is input then the extents of the Fori file will be used.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param05"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#float">float</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
					<DefaultValue>x</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Ymax</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Y maximum</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Corner coordinates of the output raster. If "x x x x" is input then the extents of the Fori file will be used.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param06"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#float">float</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
					<DefaultValue>x</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>CPix</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Side of the pixel</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Side of the pixel to get.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param07"/>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Name:PixSize"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#double">double</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Equid</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Equidistance</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>The equidistance, or vertical spacing, between curves which is used as a threshold in areas where non-linear interpolation is applied. The value should be a positive real number.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param08"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#positiveInteger">positiveInteger</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Buff</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Buffer</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Is the area of contours outside the raster that should be considered. In map units.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param09"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#positiveInteger">positiveInteger</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>PndDst</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Weighting distance</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Weighting of the distance of the curves, to give greater weight to the nearer contours. This value is used as the exponent of the inverse distance. It should be a positive real number. A value of 1.0 is recommended since this is best for calculating gradients; a value of 1.2 gives better results if we are only interested in heights. If profiles are to be given the same weight then the value selected should be 0.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param10"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#decimal">decimal</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Sing</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Processing of singular curves</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract><![CDATA[Processing of singular curves (isolated curves in the middle of planes, that often produce star-shaped effects in the interpolation) Allowed values are: <ul><li>F no special process, the fastest option.</li><li>V simple processing as a function of neighbourhood, usually an adequate option.</li><li>D process based on the previous analysis of the distances, the slowest option but usually giving the best results.</li></ul>If option D is chosen the calculations of distances to the contours and the valley lines can be avoided if they have been previously calculated with BufDist and the name of the file with the distances is given using the parameter /F_DIST=]]></ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param11"/>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>F</ows:Value>
						<ows:Value>V</ows:Value>
						<ows:Value>D</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>FTvg</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Channels ARC file</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Channels ARC file, ie drainage lines. These are usually created by digitising the rivers and streams on a topographic map.</ows:Abstract>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Tvg3D</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Channels file 3D</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Indicated when channels file contains a 3D elevation information.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>##blank##</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
					<DefaultValue>##blank##</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>FCar</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Ridges ARC file</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Ridges ARC file</ows:Abstract>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>Car3D</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Ridges 3D file</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Indicated when ridges file contains a 3D elevation information.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>##blank##</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
					<DefaultValue>##blank##</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>FBreak</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Breaklines ARC</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Breaklines ARC file, it must contain a 3D elevation information.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>##blank##</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
					<DefaultValue>##blank##</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>ENLLA_ISOL_EXT</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Criteri d'interpolació</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract><![CDATA[Indicates the interpolation criterion for the pixels that are not located between two isolines (typically the pixels beyond the exterior contour). Can take the values:<ul><li>'0' An attempt will be made to interpolate by considering, in the profiles for which the pixel is not between two contours, that the value is equal to the first contour height in the profile. The default option.<li></li>'1' Interpolation will be carried out only for values obtained in profiles in which the pixel is between two contours (the other profiles will be ignored).<li></li>'2' The affected pixels will be considered not interpolatable and will be marked as NODATA.</li></ul>]]></ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#positiveInteger">positiveInteger</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>0</ows:Value>
						<ows:Value>1</ows:Value>
						<ows:Value>2</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>BYTES_PIX</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Output raster</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Type of output raster. May be '2' (short), or '4' (float, default option).</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#positiveInteger">positiveInteger</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>2</ows:Value>
						<ows:Value>4</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>DIST_LINEAL</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Horizontal distance</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Horizontal distance beyond which non-linear interpolation is not permitted. This is very useful to avoid unusual effects in river valleys. By default the distance is 20 times the contour interval, Equid. beyond which non-linear interpolation is not permitted. This is very useful to avoid unusual effects in river valleys. By default the distance is 20 times the contour interval, Equid.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#decimal">decimal</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>F_DIST</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Distance file</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>The file obtained with BufDist containing the distances to the isolines and the valley lines. Only used when the parameter "Sing" is D.</ows:Abstract>
				<ComplexData>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>SEVER</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Severity</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Severity factor in the processing of singular contours. Should have a value >=1.0. Only used when the parameter "Sing" is V or D; The respective default values are 1.41 and 1.15. A value of 1.0 would produce the most sever results.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#double">double</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>IGNOR_PLA</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Ignore horizontal</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Ignore horizontal.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AllowedValues>
						<ows:Value>##blank##</ows:Value>
					</ows:AllowedValues>
					<DefaultValue>##blank##</DefaultValue>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>TAULA</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Table</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>When curves file is not 3D o we don't want to use 3D elevation information from that file, indicate database field with elevation values. To learn more about the values of these parameters follow the consiterations in general syntax.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
			<Input minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
				<ows:Identifier>CAMP</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Camp</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>When curves file is not 3D o we don't want to use 3D elevation information from that file, indicate database field with elevation values. To learn more about the values of these parameters follow the consiterations in general syntax.</ows:Abstract>
				<LiteralData>
					<ows:DataType ows:reference="http://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/#string">string</ows:DataType>
					<ows:AnyValue/>
				</LiteralData>
			</Input>
		</DataInputs>
		<ProcessOutputs>
			<Output>
				<ows:Identifier>FRes</ows:Identifier>
				<ows:Title>Raster output</ows:Title>
				<ows:Abstract>Is the raster output from the interpolation.</ows:Abstract>
				<ows:Metadata xlink:title="Param02"/>
				<ComplexOutput>
					<Default>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Default>
					<Supported>
						<Format>
							<MimeType>application/x-arc</MimeType>
							<Encoding>Binary</Encoding>
						</Format>
					</Supported>
				</ComplexOutput>
			</Output>
		</ProcessOutputs>
	</ProcessDescription>
</wps:ProcessDescriptions>